There are people who are on the same,

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.883
we're on the same doc with
some of our team members.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.990
Philippe, am I in the right doc?

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.890
I just typed in it, 'cause I
don't see anything coming up.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.970
Oh, there we go, we got
some questions coming up.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:28.627
So from Facebook, Luis Vega says,

00:03:28.627 --> 00:03:31.310
"Can Khan Academy reduce
the risk of infection

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.560
to our children around the world?"

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.135
Well Luis, that was quite
a question that we got

00:03:38.135 --> 00:03:41.310
to start our AMA here.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.370
So it depends on what
layer you're talking about.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.490
Obviously I don't think
Khan Academy is going

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:49.632
to prevent the infection, but if,

00:03:49.632 --> 00:03:53.640
in many parts of the world
school closures are continuing

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.570
or there's some type of hybrid learning

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.250
where kids are going to have
to learn from home online,

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.960
and that's obviously a place
where Khan Academy can keep,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.770
help keep the learning happening

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.757
while there are school closures because,

00:04:05.757 --> 00:04:07.300
and the school closures
are there, obviously,

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.470
to keep families and keep
everyone from getting sick.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.540
So I think that's Khan
Academy's primary role here,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.670
as we have a very real, legitimate role

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.340
during these school closures.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.020
We've talked about it before,
our traffic has gone up

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.110
almost by a factor of three.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.850
When schools are open,
I suspect it's going

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:25.083
to only accelerate as we
get back into school season.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.350
And then I guess there's another layer of,

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.010
an education layer that we play,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.290
whether it's this type of conversation,

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.140
bringing guests on who
can speak to the virus.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:39.060
We had Bill Gates on several months ago.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.320
We're actually going to
have several other experts

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.910
on health and vaccination
in the coming weeks.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.280
So that's another role we can play,

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.890
and obviously we can teach
people about biology,

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.370
about exponential growth, and
that can hopefully inform.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.220
But I think our biggest
role in the immediate now

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.480
is how do we keep people learning.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.931
How do we support students,
parents, and teachers

00:04:59.931 --> 00:05:03.130
during these very hard times for schools

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.893
because of what's going on?

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:10.790
So from Facebook, Sadiya Ija says,

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:15.270
"Tips to make online
teaching more interesting."

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:19.750
So great question, and I
think there's two types

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:21.580
of online teaching, at least two,

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.610
maybe there's more than two.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.350
There's what I would call
asynchronous online teaching

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.760
and then there's
synchronous online teaching.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.410
Asynchronous online teaching
is, for the most part,

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:32.640
what Khan Academy does.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.040
Students can go at
their own time and pace.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.390
They can get as much
practice, they get feedback,

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.070
there's game mechanics for them.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.820
And then if they need supports,

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:42.930
there's hints, and there's videos.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.230
And if you were thinking
about these videos,

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:46.063
which are asynchronous,

00:05:46.063 --> 00:05:48.380
I'm obviously not giving the lesson

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.220
at the exact moment that a
student is watching the lesson,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.670
there I think it's very important

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.760
for whoever is the content creator

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.720
to feel genuinely passionate

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.010
about what they are talking about,

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:03.910
to have a deep, not just interest in it,

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.780
but a deep understanding of it.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.630
Because I think if those
two things aren't true

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:12.400
when you're giving the lesson,
then I think as human beings,

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:13.550
we're very sensitive to tone.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.330
And we know that hey this
person isn't excited about it,

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.290
why should I be excited about it?

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.590
I would say another thing,

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.080
I'm not saying you cannot
make a great piece of content

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.020
if it's scripted, but once
again, human beings, students,

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.100
we're very sensitive to tone.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.960
And you usually can tell the difference

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.850
if someone is reading a script

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.900
versus if someone is actually
speaking from their mind.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.750
And when you speak from your mind,

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.270
you might say a few more ums,

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:41.490
you might pause or stutter a little bit,

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:42.870
but I actually think it's a good trade off

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.780
because people know you're a human being,

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:45.760
and you are just trying

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.470
to make transparent the thought process.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.820
So I definitely try to do
that when I make my videos.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:54.300
Many of y'all know I do them somewhat,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.780
you know I look at what the standards are,

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:59.600
what I need to cover, sometimes
I'll prepare some visuals,

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.660
or some graphics, or some
charts, some example problems,

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.700
but when I do it, I'm there,
I'm there in real time,

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.650
in the same head space as the student

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.430
while I'm recording the video.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.300
If you're doing a
synchronous online lesson,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.280
so let's say you're doing
it over Zoom, or Hangouts,

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.640
or something, like some
type of video conferencing,

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.970
there I think, well actually
some of the same things apply

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:22.910
where you know, try to
have interest in it,

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.010
try to have passion for it.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:25.420
I actually would say this would also apply

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:26.860
in a physical classroom.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.330
But I think it's that much more important

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:31.100
if you're on something like
Zoom or video conferencing

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.910
to keep it as interactive as possible.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:38.080
So it should not be the teacher, or you,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.500
just lecturing to
students for half an hour,

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:41.530
or for 30 minutes,

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:44.140
or for however long that
video conference session is.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.510
The more it can be like hey,
here's an interesting idea,

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:49.160
maybe a couple of minutes
of background or context,

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.440
and say okay, now I want all of y'all

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.310
to figure out something,
whatever subject it is.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:57.270
And obviously in some of
these video conferencing tools

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:58.820
you can put kids into breakouts.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.850
I think that is actually
the best thing to do

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:02.550
for the synchronous, 'cause
you're really leveraging

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.510
the fact that live human
beings are connecting,

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.550
at least virtually, in the moment.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:10.280
So if I was running a
Zoom session, I would say,

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:11.960
well if I were doing both,

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.040
if I were a teacher who's
having to keep kids learning

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.730
while COVID's going on and
schools are physically closed,

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.250
I would say use Khan Academy,
depending on your age,

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:22.730
some place between 30 and 60 minutes a day

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.950
to make sure that you get
all of your core skills

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:27.780
and content, that that,
you keep developing that,

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.530
and that several times a week,
maybe three times a week,

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:34.770
we'll get on video
conference for 45 minutes.

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.770
And the way that I would
run those video conferences

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:41.530
is at least two of them a week,
I would have the students,

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:42.690
I would give them some context,

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:46.080
but then I would give them a
challenging series of problems,

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.380
and maybe ones that would be challenging

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:48.850
for different levels of students,

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:51.160
and then I would ask the
students to go into breakouts

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.650
and in small groups,
try to work through it.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.530
Or maybe first, I would
say hey everyone try

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.890
to do it themselves,
come up with a solution,

00:08:57.890 --> 00:08:59.710
and then put them into breakouts

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:02.400
and try to explain their
solutions to each other,

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.430
and then come back,
and then talk about it.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:06.980
If I'm in a humanities class,

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:10.870
I would put some interesting
issue on the table,

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:15.220
as to you are Julius Caesar,
you crossed the Rubicon,

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.600
and then let the kids vote,
then let them go into breakouts,

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:20.370
let them debate with each other,

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.210
in kind of Julius Caesar's head

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:24.640
as to whether they should
cross the Rubicon or not.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.340
And then come back, and
then people kind of debrief.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.070
I think that type of
interaction will keep it

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.980
as human as possible in this online world.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.560
And frankly, everything
I just said about online,

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.560
I think is also, in a lot of ways,

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:40.173
optimal for the physical world as well.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:45.437
From Facebook, Salina Chang says,

00:09:45.437 --> 00:09:49.197
"Sal, when and how do you
structure your daily schedule?"

00:09:50.719 --> 00:09:52.050
There's two different things

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.540
that Salina might be referring to.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:56.620
She might be referring to
we have issued a daily,

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:58.850
right when COVID hit,
that was back in March,

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.790
feels like a lifetime ago,
we issued, we said look,

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.680
parents are gonna need help
structuring their days.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:06.530
Right now people are saying
here's a bunch of links,

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:08.980
make use of it, and we
said parents need help

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:10.860
knowing how to structure
their student's day

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.240
so that they can cover the material,

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.270
have time for breaks, for
play, for lunch, whatever else.

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:17.070
So we published daily schedules.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.720
The way that was created was we,

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.270
myself and several team members,

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:23.900
we said okay, let's put
all of this together.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.980
Like what are the
must-haves in a school day,

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:30.200
or in a homeschooling day, or
on a quarantine-schooling day?

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.110
How do you make sure
you have enough breaks?

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.530
How do you make sure that
you have enough free time,

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.160
time outside, but also
that you're covering

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.340
all your key subjects?

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.520
And then we've been
refining that ever since,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.710
based on feedback from
parents, and students,

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:46.460
and teachers, and education
researchers around the world.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:49.803
If Salina is asking how
do I schedule my day,

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:52.480
there's a couple things.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:55.030
So I've said multiple
times on this livestream,

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.096
I like to start with
some wins, so I always,

00:10:58.096 --> 00:11:02.730
I would say 90% of days,
I will get out of bed,

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:04.940
and I will do a meditation

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.970
for 20 to 30, sometimes 40 minutes.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.600
Then I try to do some physical exercise,

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.370
which I try to actually do a
couple of handstand pushups,

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:18.990
then I go running as fast
as I can for about a mile.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:23.990
Then I make my bed, and
sometimes, well I make my bed.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:28.540
Then I kinda do my classic brush
your teeth, et cetera stuff

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:30.100
and then I'm into my day.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:34.290
And my ideal day is that my
mornings is when I'm fresh,

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:37.090
where my brain can handle
the most cognitive load,

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:39.050
and so that's when I try
to do content creation,

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.712
'cause content creation does
take a lot of cognitive load.

00:11:41.712 --> 00:11:44.200
It's one thing to do a math problem,

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.890
or to explain some concept
in physics or the humanities,

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.104
it's another thing to do it
while you're speaking out loud,

00:11:49.104 --> 00:11:51.460
and being recorded, and
writing on a screen.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:55.250
So I try to make sure that my
brain is very fresh for that.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:57.560
And then my middle of the day,

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:00.079
I kind of have a lot of meetings.

00:12:00.079 --> 00:12:03.610
Khan Academy, we have
over 200 folks, engineers,

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:07.713
product designers, product
managers, fundraising.

00:12:08.609 --> 00:12:10.360
Relative to where it started,

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.600
it's a reasonably
complex organization now.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.252
And so wherever I can help support folks

00:12:15.252 --> 00:12:17.690
do the important work they're doing,

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:18.900
that's kinda my middle of the day.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.500
I'm also talking a lot to,
right before this I got

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.330
off the phone with a philanthropist,

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:25.790
explaining how we're running at a deficit

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.363
and that we need their support.

00:12:29.772 --> 00:12:30.890
In the middle of the day I do.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:34.050
Obviously I do the press
whenever they need me,

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:35.880
because that just helps build awareness

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.442
that we have this tool out there.

00:12:37.442 --> 00:12:40.270
Yesterday I had to wake
up at 5:00 in the morning

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.770
for Fox Business, 'cause
it was on the East Coast,

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:46.130
so doing what it takes to spread the word.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:51.130
But in my ideal day, I would
then, by the afternoon,

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:54.700
hopefully I'm just trying to
finish up all my odds and ends.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:56.410
And I just got a message
that I have something

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:57.870
on the top of my head.

00:12:57.870 --> 00:12:59.750
It does look like I do,
and I couldn't see it

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:02.973
'cause it was just this
little, oh yeah, there you go.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.523
Just a, it's a little white
piece of paper or something.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.547
Let's see, from Facebook,
Saraj Dodse says,

00:13:12.547 --> 00:13:15.787
"Is data science really a
course for a top-notch job?"

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:18.520
I would say yeah, yeah,

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:20.710
data science is a really
good skill to have,

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.341
I would say in almost any field you go to.

00:13:23.341 --> 00:13:24.890
Data science is funny.

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:27.356
I spoke at the, I forgot
what they call it,

00:13:27.356 --> 00:13:30.350
it's like in the National
Actuarial Society.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.460
And for those of you who
don't know what an actuary is,

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:37.430
an actuary, and the job of an
actuary has existed for many,

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:41.840
at least decades, maybe even a century,

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.480
frankly as long as things like insurance

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:46.210
have existed actually, so
that's probably many centuries.

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:48.960
But they are the folks
who look at the data

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:53.530
of like what's the probability
that a father of three

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:57.507
who's 43 years old, who
drives a Honda Accord,

00:13:57.507 --> 00:13:59.840
will get into a car accident,

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:02.880
and then how much would that
car accident cost on average,

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:05.930
and then based on that, what
should their car insurance be

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:08.140
so that it's profitable for the insurance,

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.170
but also something that is reasonable

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:14.130
for the father of three, which
I've just described myself.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.490
So that job is inherently data science.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:18.570
They're looking at large data sets,

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.230
and coming up with insights of it

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:24.340
in order to make kind
of business decisions.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.650
Statistics, statisticians, actuaries

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:28.500
have always been doing data science.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.760
Now with this notion of big data,

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:34.020
and you can imagine, even at Khan Academy,

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.560
we have tens of millions
of people doing exercises.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:40.070
Can we use that data in
ways that is constructive?

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:40.930
And we're very careful.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:43.070
We would never wanna use the
data, we're a non-profit,

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.360
we would never wanna use it in shady ways.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.810
But can we use it in ways
that can improve the learning,

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.570
can do better personalization?

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:54.330
Can we learn insights that
maybe certain types of concepts

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.420
are easier to learn for
certain types of students

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.050
in one way versus another way?

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.871
And so it's kind of taking
that same lens of statistics,

00:15:00.871 --> 00:15:05.630
and what actuaries do, but
with a little bit of coding

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.910
and computer programming to be able

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.580
to query these large data sets

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:12.370
and know how to structure
these large data sets,

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:17.370
and how to actually come
with real insights from them.

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:18.763
And that's going on

00:15:18.763 --> 00:15:20.230
in pretty much every
industry in the world.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:23.360
So yes, simple answer
is, if it interests you,

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:24.730
I think it's a great thing.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:28.520
And I think even if you don't
wanna become a data scientist,

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:31.360
I think it's really valuable
to become reasonably familiar

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:33.485
with it because in
pretty much any function,

00:15:33.485 --> 00:15:35.790
you're going to be asking
these types of questions.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:38.100
And maybe there's an analytics
or a data science group

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:41.730
that does some of the work,
but you need to be informed

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:45.130
in order to know how to structure
the questions correctly,

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:46.970
or to know what that information

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:48.430
is telling you or not telling you.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:50.670
So yeah, it's a good, good career

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:52.840
if it's of interest to you.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:55.637
So from Facebook, Kunza Thireek says,

00:15:55.637 --> 00:15:57.640
"How do deal with ADHD children these days

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.130
for online classes, are
there any tips for them?"

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.083
So Kunza, I'm not an expert.

00:16:03.655 --> 00:16:07.056
I'm definitely not the,
take everything I say

00:16:07.056 --> 00:16:09.670
with a super grain of salt.

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:13.320
What we've told all
parents, and I think this,

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.010
my gut sense, my intuitive sense,

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:18.640
is that this might be valuable
for students with ADHD is

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:23.680
start simple and build a habit.

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:26.570
So let's say math, or school in general.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.250
If your school, if your
child's school is closed,

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:30.690
and you're doing this
quarantine schooling,

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:33.790
you don't have to replicate
all of school, all at once.

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:34.930
That'll be overwhelming for you,

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:37.810
that'll be overwhelming for your child.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:40.113
What I would say is do
even 20 minutes a day.

00:16:41.916 --> 00:16:45.710
If attention span is an
issue, a 15 minute session,

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:47.700
a 10 minute session, sit next to them,

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:50.270
and I don't know how old
they are, but work through.

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:52.340
If they're able to do 15
minutes a day on Khan Academy,

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:53.780
maybe first thing in the morning,

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:56.750
and then create that habit,
then they're going to progress.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:00.150
And then if that feels
like that's firm footing,

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:04.610
that habit, then you could
then build on top of that.

00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:07.150
So that's my best advice is to start small

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.010
and really focus on building habits

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:12.543
versus trying to boil
the ocean all at once.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:18.057
So from Facebook, John Bernard Horn says,

00:17:18.057 --> 00:17:20.530
"Sal, how can we all
help people steer clear

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:22.670
of questionable conspiracy theories,

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:24.747
specifically regards to COVID, SARS2."

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:32.249
I mean I think the best way to steer clear

00:17:32.249 --> 00:17:35.783
of conspiracy theories are,

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:41.840
is to just be well-informed about things,

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:44.790
have good critical-thinking skills,

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:49.167
and you know, talk to people
you might not agree with,

00:17:49.167 --> 00:17:50.417
get their points of view.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:55.557
I will say that before Khan Academy,

00:17:55.557 --> 00:17:57.410
and this Khan Academy experience,

00:17:57.410 --> 00:18:01.650
you see, you read a lot about
what goes on in the world,

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:02.870
and it is sometimes tempting,

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:04.730
and this was even before social media

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:06.360
where your friend says well I'm not sure

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:10.140
if that's what really happened,
I think that person did it

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.990
so that this would happen
because they benefited from it.

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:17.241
One thing is that in my
Khan Academy experience,

00:18:17.241 --> 00:18:22.241
I've had the luxury of
meeting some of these actors,

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:24.800
some of these world leaders,
or some of these CEOs,

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:27.230
or some of these people
with a lot of resources,

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:29.410
and I have to say when
you kind of meet them,

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:32.460
and they're not, they don't have to be on,

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:35.080
and they're just kind
of being human beings,

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:38.420
I've become much more skeptical
of conspiracy theories

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:41.600
because I realize that most
people are just like trying

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:42.950
to make it through the day.

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:45.003
You might think that,

00:18:46.969 --> 00:18:48.470
and maybe what I'm about to say is scary,

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.653
that some of these intelligence
agencies and this and that,

00:18:50.653 --> 00:18:52.520
that they have these like magical pieces

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:55.740
of information and all that,
but they're running off of,

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:58.240
sometimes they find out
about stuff from the news.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.380
I've become even more skeptical
of conspiracy theories.

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:06.010
What I say is always use
critical thinking skills.

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:07.850
And look, I would say most of the time,

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:09.360
your critical thinking
skills will tell you

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:13.380
that the conspiracy theories
are not likely true,

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:14.870
but every now and then,

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.080
maybe the mainstream view isn't true.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:20.010
But that's where your
critical thinking skills

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:21.460
are going to be really, really valuable,

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.500
because yeah, I think it
is always interesting.

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:27.240
I read a lot of books, and
I like these plot twists

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:28.410
where there's like a surprise,

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:31.117
like oh you thought it was
this, but it's actually that,

00:19:31.117 --> 00:19:34.830
and you say oh well of course,
party B was the beneficiary,

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:35.760
so even though they looked

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:38.100
like the good guy, maybe they aren't.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:39.537
That's interesting, you have to,

00:19:39.537 --> 00:19:42.730
and maybe our human brain
naturally does that type of thing.

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:46.700
But you know, in some cases,
if you look at historical wars,

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:50.150
history has unearthed that sometimes

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.320
it was a false flag attack or whatever.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:55.020
Now with COVID and SARS and all that,

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:59.670
I see zero evidence, personally,
to think that it's anything

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.010
but what we are being told it is.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:06.473
It's a zoonotic virus, or at least COVID,

00:20:08.030 --> 00:20:09.410
through mutations and viruses,

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:12.993
especially RNA viruses mutate a lot,

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:17.083
and so mutated so that it
can survive in a human host.

00:20:18.497 --> 00:20:21.283
It happens to have the
properties that make it,

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:27.500
it's not so lethal, and
in the early stages,

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:29.530
and for many people, they're asymptomatic,

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:31.370
and so that's what allows it to spread,

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:34.900
but then for certain
people it is scary enough

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:36.440
and bad enough and lethal enough

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:38.964
that we all have to be very
conscientious about it,

00:20:38.964 --> 00:20:42.900
not scared, but very thoughtful
about how we make sure

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:45.660
it doesn't spread and how
we don't expose people.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:48.191
Every now and then, through
these random mutations,

00:20:48.191 --> 00:20:51.860
unfortunately it looks like
something like this shows up.

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:55.750
But who knows, but I see
no reason for anyone,

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:56.770
I would just say equip yourself

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:58.180
with critical thinking skills,

00:20:58.180 --> 00:21:00.350
don't believe everything
you see on social media.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:01.770
I have family members who forward me

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:04.150
all sorts of stuff on social media.

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:06.690
And if you just do a simple web search,

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.360
like four out of the five
things you can outright

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:11.090
just say they're false statements,

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:14.070
like you can just see
that they make no sense.

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:15.840
So I would encourage you
to do your own research

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:18.895
and talk to opposing viewpoints.

00:21:18.895 --> 00:21:23.797
So from Outdoor B Channel on YouTube,

00:21:23.797 --> 00:21:25.590
"Sal sir, what are you plans

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.737
for the future of Khan Academy?"

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:31.683
That is a big question Outdoor B.

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:35.760
So many of y'all know our
mission as a not-for-profit

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:38.660
is free world-class education
for anyone, anywhere.

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:40.090
There's three parts of that vision.

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:43.260
One is that a learner
anywhere in the world

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:45.540
should have access to world-class learning

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:48.460
as early as pre-K, all the
way through elementary,

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:49.750
middle, high school, and college,

00:21:49.750 --> 00:21:51.490
across subjects and grades,

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:53.360
in all of the world's major languages.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:56.680
So just that's a big statement,
and we're on that journey.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:58.320
The second pillar is they should be able

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:00.400
to learn that material in an engaging way

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:03.360
that's personalized to their needs,

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:06.551
and it should not be
bound by time and space.

00:22:06.551 --> 00:22:08.630
I've talked a lot about mastery learning,

00:22:08.630 --> 00:22:09.970
and that one of the main issues

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:13.030
is that kids keep moving ahead,
but they accumulate a gap.

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:16.050
They get an 80% on one
test, a 70% on another test,

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:17.480
and then all of a sudden
they're in algebra class

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:18.313
and nothing makes sense

00:22:18.313 --> 00:22:20.710
because they didn't learn
decimals well in fifth grade,

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:22.500
or negative numbers well in sixth grade.

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:26.170
And so mastery learning,
personalization helps address that.

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:27.978
And then the third pillar
of Khan Academy is that

00:22:27.978 --> 00:22:32.880
I hope that one day Khan
Academy can be used as evidence

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:35.040
of what you have mastered,

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:37.830
you could almost view it as
something of a credential.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:41.298
We had Jim Nondorf as a
guest on this livestream,

00:22:41.298 --> 00:22:43.610
the Head of Admissions
from University of Chicago,

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.953
and he said he would
love students to submit

00:22:46.953 --> 00:22:49.220
what they've mastered on Khan Academy

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.760
as evidence of not only their
academic mastery of content,

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:55.360
but also to show the admissions officers

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:57.850
that they have real determination

00:22:57.850 --> 00:22:59.200
and agency over their learning,

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:00.500
and they really can learn something

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:02.610
at their own time and pace.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:04.090
So there's that whole credentialing piece

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:05.840
that I also hope is the
future of Khan Academy.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.939
And I hope, if we go five,
10, 20 years in the future,

00:23:08.939 --> 00:23:10.580
Khan Academy is serving,

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:13.110
we have 107 million
registered users today,

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:16.140
I'm hoping that we're
serving billions of learners,

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.480
across subjects and grades,

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:19.490
in all of the world's major languages,

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:22.900
and they can point to Khan
Academy as a major reason

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:27.310
why they have become empowered
people in the world who,

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:30.040
for the most part, don't
believe in conspiracy theories.

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:32.373
(chuckling)

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:41.520
From YouTube, Aidan
Hadamee Kermansha asks,

00:23:41.677 --> 00:23:45.117
"How would you revolutionize
studying and school criteria?"

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:49.650
If I'm understanding
this question correctly,

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:51.230
what I would focus on, you know right now,

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:53.960
traditional schools, you kinda sit,

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:56.040
you kinda go through a
process, you take a test,

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.010
and whatever score you get on
the test, that is what it is.

00:23:58.010 --> 00:23:59.570
I mean if it's bad enough, you might fail,

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:02.260
but then you kind of
move onto the next thing.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:05.420
That's kind of a time-based
credentialing model.

00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:07.490
I'm a proponent, and I've advocated,

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:08.450
and I continue to advocate,

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:09.500
and it goes into the last question,

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.760
of what I would call a
competency-based model

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.310
where there's some way,
maybe an assessment,

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:17.470
could be a paper-based
assessment, online assessment,

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:19.620
in-person assessment, or some combination,

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:20.800
it could be a practical assessment

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.040
where you have to do something,
where if you can do it,

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:26.250
you get a certain cut score,
you get a certain score,

00:24:26.250 --> 00:24:28.700
you know your algebra, you know chemistry,

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:31.890
you know data science,
whatever it might be.

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:33.960
And then you have the decision

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:35.580
of the path of how to get there.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:37.230
You can do it at your own time and pace.

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:40.120
You can use Khan Academy, maybe
Khan Academy in conjunction

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:43.640
with a MOOC, it could
be in class experience,

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:44.730
it could be using a textbook,

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.040
it could be your best friend tutoring you.

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.495
What matters is that you
get to that endpoint.

00:24:49.495 --> 00:24:51.770
As soon as you get to that
competency-based model,

00:24:51.770 --> 00:24:53.250
then a lot of things change.

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:55.160
One, I think the credentials
become more powerful

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.880
because people know that
you learned the material.

00:24:57.880 --> 00:25:00.390
But then I think it
clarifies what's the role

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:01.950
of the learning experience.

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:04.730
How do you make sure that you're getting

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:07.740
to that very well-defined
outcome that matters?

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:09.850
So that would be, I
would say, the main one.

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:12.880
I would say there's other structures.

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:15.310
Learning should not be
bound by time or space.

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:16.800
We've talked about this for many years,

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:19.310
and obviously COVID is
making this a necessity,

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.460
learning should not be
bound by time and space.

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:23.153
Students should be able to
learn at their own time,

00:25:23.153 --> 00:25:24.950
in a personalized way.

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:26.570
Teachers should be empowered with data

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:28.330
so they can know what interventions

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:30.630
are gonna be most valuable
for their students.

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:35.290
Class time, or in-person, human time,

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:36.370
whether it's in a classroom,

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:38.110
or whether it's on video conferencing,

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:40.920
should be as interactive
and as active as possible.

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:44.120
It should not just be someone
lecturing or just talking.

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:48.030
It should be students working
together on interesting things

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:50.800
and pushing each other, and
answering each other's questions

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:52.220
and being able to talk to each other,

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:54.600
versus you know kind of sit there silently

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:55.770
and not saying anything.

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.720
They should either be doing
problems on their own,

00:25:57.720 --> 00:25:59.710
or working with others and collaborating,

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:02.403
including the teacher.

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:09.027
From Facebook, Eunice Bay, or Beg, Bay,

00:26:09.027 --> 00:26:10.730
"Name one teacher who
had an impact on you.

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:11.670
I am very curious."

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:13.820
From Kashmir, okay, that's great.

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:16.400
From Kashmir, what is it,
it's like middle of the night

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:18.190
in Kashmir right now.

00:26:18.190 --> 00:26:22.930
So Eunice, I can name many, many teachers.

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:27.210
I remember, I mean if
you go all the way back,

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:30.470
Miss Roberts in kindergarten,
Miss Lunda in first grade,

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:33.000
Miss Darby in second grade, and you know,

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:36.570
they were really important
because you're young,

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:37.920
but they were the first people

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:39.950
that I think I got positive feedback.

00:26:39.950 --> 00:26:42.017
I remember, they kind of said,

00:26:42.017 --> 00:26:46.154
"Hey Sal, I'm impressed
how you're doing this."

00:26:46.154 --> 00:26:48.884
It might have been like
how I colored something.

00:26:48.884 --> 00:26:51.570
The fact that I remember it
shows that it really helped

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:53.600
with kind of my self esteem.

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:56.493
And then as you get into kind
of third and fourth grade,

00:26:57.390 --> 00:26:59.380
there was Miss Krauss and Miss Rucell,

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:01.390
and this was kind of an enrichment program

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:03.566
where I remember the
first time that I went,

00:27:03.566 --> 00:27:05.510
and this was in a standard public school,

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:08.951
Metairie, Louisiana, which
is not a wealthy part

00:27:08.951 --> 00:27:11.520
of the country, for sure,

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:13.370
and Louisiana is not a wealthy state,

00:27:14.770 --> 00:27:16.880
but I remember the first time
I went into this enrichment,

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:19.487
and Miss, I think it
was Miss Rucell asked,

00:27:19.487 --> 00:27:20.830
"So Sal, what are you interested in?"

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:22.370
And I was like seven or eight years old,

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:25.350
and that was the first
time that kind of an adult

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:27.257
had genuinely, authentically said,

00:27:27.257 --> 00:27:29.080
"What are you really interested in?"

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:31.210
And when I said that I
really liked puzzles,

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:33.760
and I really liked art and drawing,

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:36.850
her response was, "Then we
will do puzzles and drawing,"

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:38.530
and that blew me away.

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:40.870
Like she asked me, I told her,

00:27:40.870 --> 00:27:43.850
and then I'm just going
to get to do more of that.

00:27:43.850 --> 00:27:47.253
I remember in second, third,
fourth grade, I would kinda,

00:27:48.487 --> 00:27:52.520
it was this enrichment
program where I got to play,

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:56.250
one I'd be doing these
deductive reasoning puzzles,

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:58.290
then I'd get to draw some days,

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:01.750
then I would get to play
chess or another game,

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:03.850
and I thought I was getting
away with something.

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:06.410
But in hindsight, sometimes
we would study art

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:09.210
or famous artists, or try
to make art in their style,

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:11.814
and this was second grade,
third grade, fourth grade,

00:28:11.814 --> 00:28:15.690
in hindsight that's some of
my best learning I ever had.

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:17.340
Some of the best learning happens

00:28:18.786 --> 00:28:21.630
when it doesn't even feel
like school, so to speak,

00:28:21.630 --> 00:28:23.790
it feels like you're doing
what you have a passion for,

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:26.770
but it's very constructive, and
you're getting better at it.

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:30.069
It was really powerful to
have kind of an adult there

00:28:30.069 --> 00:28:32.630
to encourage that type.

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:35.760
And then if you get into
fifth grade, Miss Ellis,

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:39.210
I remember she ran her
American history course

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:41.000
kind of like a college seminar.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:44.670
She would be at the front, she
would always be eating fruit,

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:46.450
and she would be like peeling
it, I have just this memory,

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:49.300
I was a fifth grader, she
would be peeling these oranges.

00:28:51.094 --> 00:28:52.240
She was very like almost

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:55.863
what you would imagine as very elegant,

00:28:58.180 --> 00:28:59.923
kind of elderly woman at the time,

00:28:59.923 --> 00:29:02.453
seemed elderly, I mean
I'm probably her age now.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.110
And she would just ask us these questions,

00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:10.180
like so we just read that
president so-and-so did this,

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:14.661
but would you have done that,
and she would make us answer,

00:29:14.661 --> 00:29:15.494
you know as fifth graders,

00:29:15.494 --> 00:29:17.240
no I think Roosevelt did the wrong thing,

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:18.510
we didn't have to expand the government,

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:19.600
say no but that was important,

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:21.130
it's what brought us
out of the Depression,

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:22.550
otherwise we would have had unemployed.

00:29:22.550 --> 00:29:24.100
And to be in a fifth grade class,

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:26.350
to have those types of
conversations, as you can tell,

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:29.030
I remembered it, and it impacted me.

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:30.640
And then as you go into middle school,

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:33.370
I remember Miss North,
actually very similar

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:36.010
to Miss Rucell in how she ran her classes,

00:29:36.010 --> 00:29:37.380
and actually very similar to Miss Ellis.

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:40.270
I still remember one of her
classes, it was an English class

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:42.750
but we spent the whole
hour talking about reality.

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:45.543
Like what is real, and
how do you know it's real?

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:49.361
That's mind blowing, it's still
mind-blowing conversation.

00:29:49.361 --> 00:29:51.603
And then as you go into high school,

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.870
there was Mister Hernandez,
who I was very close to,

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:59.210
he was kind of a sponsor
for our math team,

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:00.660
for our academic games team,

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:03.720
which I was stereotypically
very involved in,

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:05.500
probably not a surprise to y'all,

00:30:05.500 --> 00:30:06.920
but he was someone who really acted

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:08.890
as a mentor, who pushed me.

00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:10.620
When I wanted to, when I was thinking

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:11.720
about applying to college,

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:13.960
he encouraged me to think
very big, and to think,

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:18.960
you know, he's like Sal, you
could, don't put blinders on,

00:30:19.370 --> 00:30:21.680
don't just try to do what
other people are doing,

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:25.170
you can, you should think
big about your future.

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:28.640
And also Miss Kennedy who
was our journalism teacher,

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:31.373
and I was the art editor
of our school newspaper.

00:30:32.851 --> 00:30:33.880
Once again, just another,

00:30:33.880 --> 00:30:35.130
I think if there's a common theme here,

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:39.070
these are adults who are
really amazing teachers

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:43.262
who I felt didn't just see me
as another face in the class,

00:30:43.262 --> 00:30:48.262
they got to know me, they
took the time to advise me,

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:51.510
they treated me as, you
know they didn't talk down,

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:54.230
they treated me as, I
wouldn't say an equal,

00:30:54.230 --> 00:30:58.930
but someone, a mentor-mentee
type relationship,

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:00.510
which really sticks with you.

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:04.640
And actually I'd be remiss,
when I was in high school,

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:07.280
I started taking classes at
the University of New Orleans

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:09.110
when I was about 14 or 15,

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:11.620
I was taking these fancy math classes.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:13.050
I started taking multi-variable

00:31:13.050 --> 00:31:15.530
and partial-differential
equations and all this kinda stuff

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:16.660
and there was a teacher,

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:19.050
he was actually my
partial-differential equations teacher

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:22.000
at University of New Orleans,
his name was Doctor Santania.

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:25.710
I think there was some work that we needed

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:28.530
to do on a computer, and when
he found out that my family,

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:30.860
we had like no money, my mom was a cashier

00:31:30.860 --> 00:31:33.170
at a convenience store,
making minimum wage,

00:31:33.170 --> 00:31:38.170
and then we later had a
failing convenience store,

00:31:40.021 --> 00:31:41.960
and we had no computer for sure,

00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:45.690
he kinda of said look,
you've kinda got a knack

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:48.530
for this type of stuff, you
should have access to a computer

00:31:48.530 --> 00:31:50.520
and so he kinda pulled
all sorts of strings

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:51.960
for me to have access,

00:31:51.960 --> 00:31:53.920
the same access the engineering students

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:55.670
at the university had to the computers.

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:57.790
And that was the first time that I got

00:31:57.790 --> 00:32:00.480
to really program computers

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:01.700
and realize how much I enjoyed it,

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:03.040
and that obviously led

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:05.603
to me becoming a computer
science and math major.

00:32:06.697 --> 00:32:09.430
And I'm sure I'm missing
a lot of teachers,

00:32:09.430 --> 00:32:10.263
but those are the ones.

00:32:10.263 --> 00:32:12.180
You asked the question,
so I gave you a very,

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:14.530
as thorough of an answer
as I could for that.

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:16.480
I just noticed that I'm all out time.

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:17.330
These were good questions.

00:32:17.330 --> 00:32:20.690
We started slow on the questions,

00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:22.350
but it definitely picked up,

00:32:22.350 --> 00:32:23.340
and now there's a ton of questions.

00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:24.550
I think next time if we start slow,

00:32:24.550 --> 00:32:26.046
I'll just get back to
some of these questions

00:32:26.046 --> 00:32:27.610
that I have right here.

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:29.810
But I really appreciate all the questions.

00:32:29.810 --> 00:32:33.020
And tomorrow, we're going
to have Jacquelline Fuller,

00:32:33.020 --> 00:32:36.530
who is the president of Google.org,

00:32:36.530 --> 00:32:40.470
which is the arm of Google
that is philanthropic

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:41.590
and doing what it can.

00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:43.670
And I would say Google generally is trying

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:45.320
to do what it can to,

00:32:45.320 --> 00:32:47.610
I guess organize the world's
information or knowledge.

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:51.080
But Google.org is very
much interested in things

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:52.980
in kind of the not-for-profit
philanthropic sector,

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:54.830
and actually they've
been big funders of ours.

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:56.110
I'm eager to talk to Jacquelline,

00:32:56.110 --> 00:32:58.550
who I consider a good friend,

00:32:58.550 --> 00:33:02.410
on what's on their plate because of COVID

00:33:02.410 --> 00:33:03.960
and everything else in the world,

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:06.870
and how we can collectively
help address those issues.

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:09.703
So I look forward to seeing
all of y'all tomorrow.

00:33:24.222 --> 00:33:27.139
(electronic music)

